    "['set protocols bfd peer [peer_ip]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BFD peer configuration:\\n\\n##\\nbfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3\\nbfd map ipv4 10.1.1.2/32 GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The BFD parameters (interval, min_rx, multiplier) are set globally in Cisco, while Juniper sets them per-interface\\n2. Cisco requires the subnet mask (/32 in this case) in the BFD map command\\n3. The interface naming follows Cisco's format (GigabitEthernetX/Y/Z)\", 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd map ipv4 10.1.1.2/32 GigabitEthernet0/0/0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd map ipv4 10.1.1.2/32 GigabitEthernet0/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BFD peer configuration:\\n\\n##\\nbfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3\\nbfd map ipv4 10.1.1.2/32 GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The BFD parameters (interval, min_rx, multiplier) are set globally in Cisco, while Juniper sets them per-interface\\n2. Cisco requires the subnet mask (/32 in this case) in the BFD map command\\n3. The interface naming follows Cisco's format (GigabitEthernetX/Y/Z)\", 'trans_template': 'bfd map ipv4 10.1.1.2/32 GigabitEthernet0/0/0'}]]": 1,
