    "['set protocols bfd peer [peer_ip]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nbfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3\\nbfd map ipv4 10.2.1.2/32 gigabitEthernet 0/0/1 10.2.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco command combines both the BFD parameters (from the earlier Juniper interface configuration) and the peer mapping.\\n2. The IP address after the interface (10.2.1.1) is the local interface address that would be configured on the Cisco router's interface.\\n3. The /32 mask is used in the BFD map command as this is a point-to-point neighbor mapping.\", 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd map ipv4 10.2.1.2/32 gigabitEthernet 0/0/1 10.2.1.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd map ipv4 10.2.1.2/32 gigabitEthernet 0/0/1 10.2.1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nbfd interval 100 min_rx 100 multiplier 3\\nbfd map ipv4 10.2.1.2/32 gigabitEthernet 0/0/1 10.2.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. The Cisco command combines both the BFD parameters (from the earlier Juniper interface configuration) and the peer mapping.\\n2. The IP address after the interface (10.2.1.1) is the local interface address that would be configured on the Cisco router's interface.\\n3. The /32 mask is used in the BFD map command as this is a point-to-point neighbor mapping.\", 'trans_template': 'bfd map ipv4 10.2.1.2/32 gigabitEthernet 0/0/1 10.2.1.1'}]]": 1,
